@meta
id: 12-10
title: When case matters and when it does not
chapter: 12
type: lesson
skills: search-slash
par: 36
minutes: 5
vscode: the Aa button in the find box
vimrc: set ignorecase smartcase
open: app.log

@brief
This log shouts its levels: `INFO`, `WARN`, `ERROR`. Exactly one line
carries the word `error` in lower case, inside a gateway response body,
and that is the line that needs following up. Append ` escalated=true` to
it.

Searching for `error` here finds thirteen shouted ones first, because of
two settings almost everyone runs with: `ignorecase` makes searches
case-blind, and `smartcase` makes them case-sensitive again the moment
you type a capital yourself. Together they do the right thing perhaps
nine times in ten. This is the tenth, where you want lower case
specifically and the settings are against you.

There is a way to say "this pattern, case exactly as written" without
changing any setting.

@keys
| `/error` | with `ignorecase`, matches `error`, `Error` and `ERROR` |
| `/Error` | with `smartcase`, a capital in the pattern means case matters |
| `/\Cerror` | force case-sensitive, wherever it appears in the pattern |
| `/\cerror` | force case-insensitive, the other way round |
| `:set ic` / `:set noic` / `:set scs` | the settings themselves |

@hints
The plain search finds the uppercase levels because case is being
ignored. You need to overrule that for this one search — not for the
session.
--
`\C` anywhere in a pattern forces the whole pattern to be
case-sensitive. Its lower-case twin `\c` forces the opposite. Neither
changes a setting, so the next search behaves normally again.
--
`/\Cerror` `Enter` goes straight to the only lower-case one. Then `A`
and the text you are appending.

@solution
/\Cerror<Enter>  A escalated=true<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Thirty keystrokes, and seventeen of them are the text you are adding.

The settings are worth understanding because you will inherit them from
every vimrc you ever copy. `ignorecase` on its own is annoying: you can
never search for a constant like `ERROR` specifically. `smartcase` fixes
that by watching what you type — an all-lower-case pattern is
case-blind, a pattern with any capital in it is not. So `/error` finds
everything and `/Error` finds only `Error`. That is the behaviour you
want by default.

What `smartcase` cannot express is this lesson's case: lower case, and
only lower case. Typing a capital is how you ask for precision, so there
is no way to ask for precision about lower case by typing. `\C` is the
escape hatch, and it works from either direction — `\c` forces a
case-blind search in a file where you have case sensitivity on.

Both are part of the pattern, not commands, so they travel: `\C` works
in `:substitute`, in `:global`, in `:vimgrep`, everywhere a pattern is
accepted. That is the first hint of something chapter 13 leans on
heavily — search patterns are a small language, and the things you learn
about them are reusable across half of vim.

